Score 2 | Comments 0 (<a href="https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=36634744">thread link</a>) | @badrequest
<br/>
July 7, 2023 | https://blog.dinnerdonebetter.dev/post/wasm-lesson-restraint/ | <a href="https://web.archive.org/web/*/https://blog.dinnerdonebetter.dev/post/wasm-lesson-restraint/">archive.org</a>
<br/><!-- hnss:readable-content --><hr/><div id="readability-page-1" class="page"><article>
<header><p><a href="https://blog.dinnerdonebetter.dev/post/wasm-lesson-restraint/"><img src="https://blog.dinnerdonebetter.dev/post/wasm-lesson-restraint/cover_hufd334e3919f8eb4ce4d87476202c812d_2040245_800x0_resize_box_3.png" srcset="https://blog.dinnerdonebetter.dev/post/wasm-lesson-restraint/cover_hufd334e3919f8eb4ce4d87476202c812d_2040245_800x0_resize_box_3.png 800w, https://blog.dinnerdonebetter.dev/post/wasm-lesson-restraint/cover_hufd334e3919f8eb4ce4d87476202c812d_2040245_1600x0_resize_box_3.png 1600w" width="800" height="800" loading="lazy" alt="Featured image of post Tale of a mistake"></a></p><div><header><a href="https://blog.dinnerdonebetter.dev/categories/eng/">engineering</a></header><div><h2><a href="https://blog.dinnerdonebetter.dev/post/wasm-lesson-restraint/">Tale of a mistake</a></h2><h3>Everyone makes mistakes, but I seem to keep making the one where I try to make use of WebAssembly</h3></div></div></header><section><h2 id="background">Background</h2><p>Dinner Done Better’s recipe model effectively the recipe as a list of steps, and allows for relationships between these steps. If you mince onions in step #1 to produce minced onions, you can specify that those minced onions are an ingredient in the production of step #2.</p><p>As such, every recipe in Dinner Done Better compiles down to a <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Directed_acyclic_graph" target="_blank" rel="noopener">DAG</a>. Over the course of building this app, I’ve tried to find new ways to utilize this DAG to inform food preparation, from generating GraphViz flowcharts of recipes, to ensuring that submitted recipes actually are acyclic. I’ve built functions that do things like look for long branches in the DAG to identify them as steps primed for ahead-of-time preparation. All of the DAG tooling I’ve written for recipes has been written in Go, because it’s the language I’m most familiar in. In my head, when I write pseudocode, I write Go.</p><p>When it came time to build a frontend, I wanted the quickest and easiest way possible to render a flowchart in React, and somehow landed on <a href="https://reactflow.dev/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ReactFlow</a>. ReactFlow is incredible software, even the stuff that’s available for free, but unfortunately the most incredible parts are locked behind <a href="https://pro.reactflow.dev/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">a $139/month (at the time of this writing) license</a>, which is significantly higher than literally every other expense I have related to this project combined. So rather than further investigate how I could make better use of ReactFlow, I started looking for alternatives. Separately, I had discovered <a href="https://mermaid.js.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Mermaid</a> via it’s <a href="https://github.blog/2022-02-14-include-diagrams-markdown-files-mermaid/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">built-in support on GitHub</a>, and it wasn’t long before I thought I should replace ReactFlow with it.</p><h2 id="starting-work">Starting work</h2><p>Like I said, when I start to write code I’ve not ever really written before, I tend to do it in Go. Not to mention that I already had code for iterating over the recipe the way I knew I was going to need to, so I built a quick function that generated a Mermaid flowchart from it. I wanted to quickly see these results on the website, so I slapped a couple of tests on my prototype, and put it into an HTTP route handler, such that I could just add <code>/mermaid</code> to any recipe URL and get the mermaid representation for it.</p><p>As I went to implement its use on the frontend side of it, something started to bug me. You see, the web app is a server-side rendered Next app, and the recipe page works by first fetching data from the API, and then renders the webpage with the retrieved information. The API route for getting the recipe reads that data from the database. The API route that renders a recipe to Mermaid also has to fetch a recipe. So if I added another call to also get the mermaid representation of a recipe, I would be asking the API server to fetch the same recipe twice per page load. Asinine.</p><h2 id="the-dilemma-right-under-my-nose">The dilemma right under my nose</h2><p>I had to make a choice from a few options:</p><ol><li>Re-write my Go code in Typescript.<ul><li>This is the good I made an enemy of for perfection. If you take nothing else from this blog post, remember at least that you should do a quick and cheap port when the opportunity presents itself.</li></ul></li><li>Add a field for the Mermaid chart to the standard recipe response<ul><li>I disagree with the notion that API servers should exist solely to serve the whims of websites. You should be able to only need recipe data without having to also tolerate its Mermaid representation.</li></ul></li><li>Adapt my Go code into a library of WASM helpers that I serve from the API server and then make use of on the frontend.<ul><li><em>*sigh*</em> …this is what I did.</li></ul></li></ol><p>I thought I had the perfect storm: code I didn’t want to throw away on the backend, but that needed to be used on the frontend, too. I should have recognized this for what it was: an validated algorithm I could very easily port from Go to Typescript as necessary. Instead, I let my impulses convince myself that this was precisely when one should incorporate <a href="https://webassembly.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">WASM</a>.</p><p>I’m not going to go into WASM, the myriad smarter people in the world have already done so, to great effect.</p><h2 id="going-for-it">Going for it</h2><p>It took me about a day or so of effort to get everything functioning such that I could anticipate its utility. A particular pain for me was that Next has a whole bunch of special rules about scripts (for good reasons) which threw me for a loop. Go requires <a href="https://go.googlesource.com/go.git/+/refs/tags/go1.20.1/misc/wasm/wasm_exec.js" target="_blank" rel="noopener">a lengthy javscript file</a> to be loaded on your page to get it to work properly, and once you do that, the only way I could manage to propagate my WASM helper functions down the line such that my components could invoke them was to attach them to the window object. Testing that locally worked for me, so I knew I needed the end webpage to boil down to this:</p><div><div><table><tbody><tr><td><pre tabindex="0"><code><span>1
</span><span>2
</span><span>3
</span><span>4
</span><span>5
</span><span>6
</span><span>7
</span><span>8
</span><span>9
</span></code></pre></td><td><pre tabindex="0"><code data-lang="html"><span><span><span>&lt;</span><span>script</span> <span>src</span><span>=</span><span>"/wasm/exec.js"</span><span>&gt;&lt;/</span><span>script</span><span>&gt;</span>
</span></span><span><span><span>&lt;</span><span>script</span><span>&gt;</span>
</span></span><span><span>    <span>const</span> <span>go</span> <span>=</span> <span>new</span> <span>Go</span><span>();</span>
</span></span><span><span>    <span>WebAssembly</span><span>.</span><span>instantiateStreaming</span><span>(</span><span>fetch</span><span>(</span><span>"/wasm/helpers"</span><span>),</span> <span>go</span><span>.</span><span>importObject</span><span>).</span><span>then</span><span>((</span><span>result</span><span>)</span> <span>=&gt;</span> <span>{</span>
</span></span><span><span>        <span>go</span><span>.</span><span>run</span><span>(</span><span>result</span><span>.</span><span>instance</span><span>);</span>
</span></span><span><span>
</span></span><span><span>        <span>window</span><span>.</span><span>renderRecipeToMermaid</span> <span>=</span> <span>renderRecipeToMermaid</span>
</span></span><span><span>    <span>});</span>
</span></span><span><span><span>&lt;/</span><span>script</span><span>&gt;</span>
</span></span></code></pre></td></tr></tbody></table></div></div><p>Figuring out how to get Next to generate that equivalent in <code>_document.tsx</code> felt esoteric. The browser would complain that it couldn’t instantiate a new instance of Go and there was basically no help troubleshooting online for this particular combination of technologies. Eventually I sussed out that I needed to specify that the scripts get loaded <code>beforeInteractive</code>, and the instantiations that rely on those scripts being loaded should get loaded <code>afterInteractive</code>, like so:</p><div><div><table><tbody><tr><td><pre tabindex="0"><code><span> 1
</span><span> 2
</span><span> 3
</span><span> 4
</span><span> 5
</span><span> 6
</span><span> 7
</span><span> 8
</span><span> 9
</span><span>10
</span><span>11
</span></code></pre></td><td><pre tabindex="0"><code data-lang="typescript"><span><span><span>import</span> <span>Script</span> <span>from</span> <span>'next/script'</span>
</span></span><span><span>
</span></span><span><span><span>&lt;</span><span>Script</span> <span>src</span><span>=</span><span>"/wasm/exec.js"</span> <span>strategy</span><span>=</span><span>"beforeInteractive"</span> <span>/&gt;</span>
</span></span><span><span><span>&lt;</span><span>Script</span> <span>strategy</span><span>=</span><span>"afterInteractive"</span><span>&gt;</span>
</span></span><span><span>    <span>const</span> <span>go</span> <span>=</span> <span>new</span> <span>Go</span><span>();</span>
</span></span><span><span>    <span>WebAssembly</span><span>.</span><span>instantiateStreaming</span><span>(</span><span>fetch</span><span>(</span><span>"/wasm/helpers"</span><span>),</span> <span>go</span><span>.</span><span>importObject</span><span>).</span><span>then</span><span>((</span><span>result</span><span>)</span> <span>=&gt;</span> <span>{</span>
</span></span><span><span>        <span>go</span><span>.</span><span>run</span><span>(</span><span>result</span><span>.</span><span>instance</span><span>);</span>
</span></span><span><span>
</span></span><span><span>        <span>window</span><span>.</span><span>renderRecipeToMermaid</span> <span>=</span> <span>renderRecipeToMermaid</span><span>;</span>
</span></span><span><span>    <span>});</span>
</span></span><span><span><span>&lt;/</span><span>Script</span><span>&gt;</span>
</span></span></code></pre></td></tr></tbody></table></div></div><p>With that, everything worked. While I did get it to work, it did so in such a way that I felt like I was making my product worse.</p><p>While I didn’t have the foresight to recognize an opportunity to do less work before, I owed it to myself to recognize that opportunity now. I decided it would be significantly easier to simply port the Go code over to Typescript <em>and</em> delete all the WASM code I’d written, than it would be to support or maintain this stuff with confidence in the future.</p><p>I had the code I needed to be written done in 20 minutes, and the rest of the code deleted in another 10, and I’m a happy camper.</p><p>Here’s what <a href="https://app.dinnerdonebetter.dev/recipes/cgfpaoa23akg00ad6ac0" target="_blank" rel="noopener">One-Pot Spaghetti and Kale</a>’s recipe DAG looks like, by the way:</p><pre>%%{
  init: {
    'theme': 'base',
    'themeVariables': {
        'background': '#FFF',
        'primaryColor': '#FFF',
        'primaryTextColor': '#666',
        'noteTextColor': '#666',
        'noteBkgColor': '#272822',
        'primaryBorderColor': '#000',
        'noteBorderColor': '#000',
        'lineColor': '#8c53c6',
        'secondaryColor': '#FFF',
        'clusterBkg': '#272822',
        'titleColor': '#FFF'
    }
